Patch 29 -- stop issuing GL state that a GLES driver rejects.

Found by reading the active patches, then measured on the device. With err+d3d
enabled, a two-minute run of the production build reports 52,365 rejected GL
calls through wined3d_check_gl_call. Two sources are named exactly here:

  9,938  GL_INVALID_ENUM from depth_clip        (ffp_gl.c)
  9,938  GL_INVALID_ENUM from glDisable(GL_LINE_SMOOTH)

depth_clip() already guards on gl_info->supported[ARB_DEPTH_CLAMP], so the bug is
upstream of it: ARB_DEPTH_CLAMP is credited by the desktop version table at GL
3.2, and this driver reports "OpenGL ES 3.2" -- which patch 6 now parses
correctly. Desktop 3.2 has GL_DEPTH_CLAMP, GLES 3.2 does not. The CrossOver
Android capability-clearing list covers twenty such flags but not this one, so
the fix belongs in that list.

line_antialias() has no capability bit to clear: GLES has no line smoothing at
all, so the guard has to live at the call. state_msaa() is the same class --
GL_MULTISAMPLE does not exist in GLES, where multisampling is a property of the
framebuffer. ARB_MULTISAMPLE is deliberately NOT cleared, because the samples
themselves are real and other code depends on that bit; only the toggle is
meaningless.

In production these errors are invisible, since ERR sits behind a channel check
and WINEDEBUG is -all. The calls happen regardless and the driver discards them:
roughly 165 rejected round trips per second, one pair per rasterizer state
application. Removing them cannot change what the driver was going to do.

Note on the third source in that run, 21,537 GL_INVALID_OPERATION attributed to
"glEnable GL_SAMPLE_ALPHA_TO_COVERAGE": that enum is core in GLES 3.0, so the
call is legal and the error was almost certainly pending from an earlier
unchecked call -- checkGLcall prints whatever glGetError returns, not necessarily
the culprit. Not addressed here; naming its real source needs a run with
glGetError before the call, not more reading.

--- a/dlls/wined3d/adapter_gl.c
+++ b/dlls/wined3d/adapter_gl.c
@@ -3589,6 +3589,14 @@
         gl_info->supported[ARB_VERTEX_PROGRAM] = FALSE;
         gl_info->supported[ARB_FRAGMENT_PROGRAM] = FALSE;
         gl_info->supported[ARB_DEBUG_OUTPUT] = FALSE;
+        /* ARB_DEPTH_CLAMP is credited purely by version: the table above lists it
+         * at GL 3.2 and this driver reports "OpenGL ES 3.2", which opengl32 now
+         * parses correctly. Desktop 3.2 has GL_DEPTH_CLAMP, GLES 3.2 does not, so
+         * depth_clip() passed its supported[] guard and then issued an enum the
+         * driver rejects. Measured on the device 2026-08-13: 9,938 rejected calls
+         * from depth_clip in a two-minute run, one per rasterizer state
+         * application, every one of them discarded by the driver. */
+        gl_info->supported[ARB_DEPTH_CLAMP] = FALSE;
         gl_info->supported[ARB_DRAW_ELEMENTS_BASE_VERTEX] = FALSE;
         gl_info->supported[ARB_MAP_BUFFER_ALIGNMENT] = FALSE;
         gl_info->supported[ARB_POINT_SPRITE] = FALSE;
--- a/dlls/wined3d/ffp_gl.c
+++ b/dlls/wined3d/ffp_gl.c
@@ -874,6 +874,14 @@
